      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Washington,_D.C./Shaw",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The neighborhood grew from freed‑slave encampments and became a pre‑Harlem hub for African‑American intellectual and cultural life.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        27,
        28,
        29
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shaw_(Washington%2C_D.C.)",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "CityMarket on O Street, a Gothic Revival landmark, opened in the 1920s and was added to the National Register of Historic Places in 1968.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1,
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shaw_(Washington%2C_D.C.)",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Shaw’s music scene thrived with venues like Howard Theater (1910), Lincoln Theatre (1921), and Bohemian Caverns jazz club (1926).",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        18
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
